        "notes": "The star in the color of its spectrum, with its broad belt of dust in the belt's own glow at 1.3 millimetres, as ALMA imaged it in April and May 2015 (project 2013.1.00612.S). It is the ARI-L project's image from the public archive, brought to the resolution of the map Su et al. (2017) made from these and a second set of observations, and shown in the color scale of their map of the disc alone. It holds half their data and is noisier. Two sources behind the ring are removed as the points Su et al. subtract, and sky fainter than three times the image noise fades out. The brightest spot, on the ring's west side, is what is left of the brighter of those sources, which Su et al. find consistent with a distant galaxy; it is in their map too and is not known to be dust. The ring's size and tilt are Su et al.'s fit: brightest about 200 au from the star, 168 au wide, tilted 30°. The image has no third axis, so the depth is inferred: a tilted ring projects to it better than a spherical shell or a constant depth. Its thickness and which side is nearer are conventions. Planet b orbits inside the ring.",
